        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4001ad1a-ffa4-5e87-ab3f-94d1e9cdae24",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: International Ultraviolet Explorer, EXOSAT\n- Organizations: European Space Astronomy Centre, European Space Agency\n- People: Laurence O'Rourke\n- Places: European Space Astronomy Centre\n- Topics: space astronomy missions, scientific archives, asteroid naming\n\nFacts:\n- The International Ultraviolet Explorer was an astronomical observatory satellite primarily designed to take ultraviolet spectra.\n- The International Ultraviolet Explorer was initially planned for a 3-year mission, but it lasted almost 18 years before being shut down in 1996.\n- EXOSAT, originally named HELOS, was a European X-ray Observatory Satellite that operated from May 1983 until April 1986.\n- During its operations from 1983 to 1986, EXOSAT made 1,780 X-ray observations of many classes of astronomical objects, including active galactic nuclei, stellar coronae, cataclysmic variables, white dwarfs, X-ray binaries, clusters of galaxies, and supernova remnants.\n- The European Space Astronomy Centre hosts the science operation centres for all European Space Agency astronomy and planetary missions together with their scientific archives.\n- Past and present missions represented at the European Space Astronomy Centre include Akari, Cassini–Huygens, Cluster, Exomars, Gaia, Herschel, Hubble, ISO, INTEGRAL, IUE, LISA Pathfinder, Mars Express, Planck, Rosetta, SOHO, Venus Express, and XMM-Newton.\n- The asteroid 9524 O'Rourke was named after Laurence O'Rourke, a researcher at the European Space Astronomy Centre.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "810b7132-e9b3-5ef7-b276-34181f4f8924",
        "text": "Page: International Ultraviolet Explorer. Sentence: The International Ultraviolet Explorer (IUE) was an astronomical observatory satellite primarily designed to take ultraviolet spectra.\nPage: International Ultraviolet Explorer. Sentence: The mission lifetime was initially set for 3 years, but in the end it lasted almost 18 years, with the satellite being shut down in 1996.\nPage: EXOSAT. Sentence: The European X-ray Observatory Satellite (EXOSAT), originally named HELOS, was an X-ray telescope operational from May 1983 until April 1986 and in that time made 1780 observations in the X-ray band of most classes of astronomical object including active galactic nuclei, stellar coronae, cataclysmic variables, white dwarfs, X-ray binaries, clusters of galaxies, and supernova remnants.\nPage: European Space Astronomy Centre. Sentence: It hosts the science operation centres for all ESA astronomy and planetary missions together with their scientific archives.Past and present missions represented at ESAC include (in alphabetical order) Akari, Cassini–Huygens, Cluster, Exomars, Gaia, Herschel, Hubble, ISO, INTEGRAL, IUE, LISA Pathfinder, Mars Express, Planck, Rosetta, SOHO, Venus Express, and XMM-Newton.\nPage: 9524 O'Rourke. Sentence: The asteroid was named after Laurence O'Rourke, a researcher at the European Space Astronomy Centre.",
